1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,070
January seventh, nineteen forty-eight.

2
00:00:03,070 --> 00:00:12,216
Captain Thomas Mantell, twenty-five years old, a decorated war hero, climbs into a fighter plane to chase something in the sky.

3
00:00:12,216 --> 00:00:14,233
Within the hour, he is dead.

4
00:00:14,699 --> 00:00:18,541
The newspapers said he died chasing a flying saucer.

5
00:00:18,541 --> 00:00:23,933
It was the first time anyone had lost their life, so it seemed, to a UFO.

6
00:00:24,399 --> 00:00:25,836
So tonight, we climb.

7
00:00:25,836 --> 00:00:31,856
We follow the flight rung by rung, from the ground to the last altitude he ever reached.

8
00:00:31,856 --> 00:00:34,866
Every claim gets checked against the record.

9
00:00:35,333 --> 00:00:39,173
At each height, we ask what is evidence, and what is rumor.

10
00:00:39,173 --> 00:00:44,899
Then we look at the suspects: a planet, a reflection, a secret balloon, and a spaceship.

11
00:00:45,366 --> 00:00:46,806
At the end, a verdict.

12
00:00:46,806 --> 00:00:50,666
Let's start on the ground, at one fifteen in the afternoon.

13
00:00:55,133 --> 00:00:57,267
One fifteen in the afternoon.

14
00:00:57,267 --> 00:01:03,303
In the control tower at Godman Field, beside Fort Knox, Kentucky, the phone rings.

15
00:01:03,303 --> 00:01:05,585
It is the state highway patrol.

16
00:01:05,585 --> 00:01:09,633
People near Maysville are reporting a strange aircraft.

17
00:01:10,099 --> 00:01:14,523
Godman checks with flight service at Wright Field in Ohio.

18
00:01:14,523 --> 00:01:15,362
No traffic.

19
00:01:15,362 --> 00:01:19,176
About twenty minutes later, the police call again.

20
00:01:19,176 --> 00:01:23,066
Now it is Owensboro and Irvington, far to the west.

21
00:01:23,533 --> 00:01:33,094
Those towns describe something circular, about two hundred and fifty to three hundred feet across, moving west at, quote, a pretty good clip.

22
00:01:33,094 --> 00:01:35,400
The report is passed to Fort Knox.

23
00:01:35,866 --> 00:01:38,734
At one forty-five, the tower sees it too.

24
00:01:38,734 --> 00:01:44,049
The assistant operator watches it for several minutes before he says a word.

25
00:01:44,049 --> 00:01:47,966
He does not want to, quote, make a flying saucer report.

26
00:01:48,433 --> 00:01:49,835
The chief is called.

27
00:01:49,835 --> 00:01:51,798
Then the operations officer.

28
00:01:51,798 --> 00:01:53,901
Then the intelligence officer.

29
00:01:53,901 --> 00:01:56,776
Then the base commander, Colonel Guy Hix.

30
00:01:56,776 --> 00:02:00,001
Each looks through the tower's big binoculars.

31
00:02:00,001 --> 00:02:01,333
Nobody can name it.

32
00:02:01,799 --> 00:02:03,955
They say it is not an airplane.

33
00:02:03,955 --> 00:02:06,527
They say it is not a weather balloon.

34
00:02:06,527 --> 00:02:08,265
One calls it a parachute.

35
00:02:08,265 --> 00:02:11,533
Another says an ice cream cone tipped with red.

36
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,072
Others say round and white.

37
00:02:14,072 --> 00:02:15,300
Huge and silver.

38
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:18,139
One fourth the size of the full moon.

39
00:02:18,139 --> 00:02:19,674
A small white thing.

40
00:02:19,674 --> 00:02:21,669
The descriptions disagree.

41
00:02:21,669 --> 00:02:25,199
But almost all of them describe something big.

42
00:02:25,666 --> 00:02:30,499
Around two thirty, four fighter planes come into view from the south.

43
00:02:30,499 --> 00:02:33,651
The National Guard, flying home from Georgia.

44
00:02:33,651 --> 00:02:36,733
The tower calls the leader, Captain Mantell.

45
00:02:37,199 --> 00:02:38,889
Mantell is twenty-five.

46
00:02:38,889 --> 00:02:44,911
In the war, he flew a transport plane on D-Day and towed gliders under heavy fire.

47
00:02:44,911 --> 00:02:47,922
He earned the Distinguished Flying Cross.

48
00:02:47,922 --> 00:02:50,933
He has a wife, Peggy, and two small sons.

49
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:55,032
One of the four is low on fuel and flies on home.

50
00:02:55,032 --> 00:02:59,925
Mantell takes his two wingmen, turns, and heads toward the object.

51
00:02:59,925 --> 00:03:01,630
The tower directs them.

52
00:03:01,630 --> 00:03:04,966
At first, none of the pilots can even see it.

53
00:03:05,433 --> 00:03:06,281
They climb.

54
00:03:06,281 --> 00:03:13,141
The fighter is a North American F-51, the same plane the world knows as the P-51 Mustang.

55
00:03:13,141 --> 00:03:19,000
It is fast, and it is powerful, but it is built for low and middle altitude.

56
00:03:19,466 --> 00:03:23,847
By ten thousand feet, the two wingmen see Mantell pulling away.

57
00:03:23,847 --> 00:03:27,533
He is ahead, climbing hard, and just barely in sight.

58
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,557
Then, around two forty-five, his voice on the radio.

59
00:03:31,557 --> 00:03:36,278
Quote, I see something above and ahead of me, and I'm still climbing.

60
00:03:36,278 --> 00:03:38,400
Everyone in the tower hears it.

61
00:03:38,866 --> 00:03:43,378
One of the wingmen answers with a question that survives in the record.

62
00:03:43,378 --> 00:03:45,474
What the hell are we looking for?

63
00:03:45,474 --> 00:03:47,000
They cannot see a thing.

64
00:03:47,466 --> 00:03:50,362
The tower asks Mantell for a description.

65
00:03:50,362 --> 00:03:52,763
And here the story starts to bend.

66
00:03:52,763 --> 00:03:56,647
Nobody in the tower can later remember his exact words.

67
00:03:56,647 --> 00:03:59,966
And, despite the rumors, there is no recording.

68
00:04:00,433 --> 00:04:04,135
Saucer writers later credited him with a famous line.

69
00:04:04,135 --> 00:04:07,907
Quote, it looks metallic, and it's tremendous in size.

70
00:04:07,907 --> 00:04:10,212
The line was repeated everywhere.

71
00:04:10,212 --> 00:04:12,866
But the tower never agreed he said it.

72
00:04:13,333 --> 00:04:17,733
What the tower did hear, all of them, was the last clear sentence.

73
00:04:17,733 --> 00:04:20,466
Quote, I'm going to twenty thousand feet.

74
00:04:20,933 --> 00:04:23,174
Now the rule that matters most.

75
00:04:23,174 --> 00:04:26,934
Every pilot in those days was taught the same thing.

76
00:04:26,934 --> 00:04:30,766
Do not go above fifteen thousand feet without oxygen.

77
00:04:31,233 --> 00:04:39,366
Captain Edward Ruppelt, who later ran the Air Force's UFO project, took the training himself, in a pressure chamber.

78
00:04:39,366 --> 00:04:43,643
A few tough men reached fifteen thousand feet without a mask.

79
00:04:43,643 --> 00:04:45,466
Nobody got past seventeen.

80
00:04:45,933 --> 00:04:48,361
Lack of oxygen is called hypoxia.

81
00:04:48,361 --> 00:04:49,243
It is quiet.

82
00:04:49,243 --> 00:04:51,598
First you feel fine, even happy.

83
00:04:51,598 --> 00:04:53,363
Then your judgment goes.

84
00:04:53,363 --> 00:04:54,614
Then your vision.

85
00:04:54,614 --> 00:04:58,366
Then you black out, and you never notice it coming.

86
00:04:58,833 --> 00:05:01,358
The wingmen carry no oxygen either.

87
00:05:01,358 --> 00:05:03,954
At fifteen thousand feet, they stop.

88
00:05:03,954 --> 00:05:05,253
They call Mantell.

89
00:05:05,253 --> 00:05:05,974
No answer.

90
00:05:05,974 --> 00:05:08,066
They start to come back down.

91
00:05:08,533 --> 00:05:11,308
One of them says something on the way home.

92
00:05:11,308 --> 00:05:14,988
All he had seen, he says, was a reflection on his canopy.

93
00:05:14,988 --> 00:05:16,666
We will come back to that.

94
00:05:17,133 --> 00:05:18,821
Mantell goes on alone.

95
00:05:18,821 --> 00:05:20,125
Fifteen thousand.

96
00:05:20,125 --> 00:05:20,738
Sixteen.

97
00:05:20,738 --> 00:05:21,429
Eighteen.

98
00:05:21,429 --> 00:05:21,966
Twenty.

99
00:05:21,966 --> 00:05:27,566
He is chasing something above him that, as he flies, seems to climb away.

100
00:05:28,033 --> 00:05:30,749
Back at Godman, the radio goes quiet.

101
00:05:30,749 --> 00:05:33,024
Calls to Mantell get no answer.

102
00:05:33,024 --> 00:05:35,666
He will never speak to anyone again.

103
00:05:36,133 --> 00:05:38,157
Why would a careful pilot do it?

104
00:05:38,157 --> 00:05:43,915
A friend who flew with him for years said Mantell was one of the most cautious men he knew.

105
00:05:43,915 --> 00:05:49,166
His guess was that he was after something he believed more important than his life.

106
00:05:49,633 --> 00:05:51,606
Or maybe the plan was simple.

107
00:05:51,606 --> 00:05:57,457
Climb fast to twenty thousand, take a look, drop back down before the oxygen runs out.

108
00:05:57,457 --> 00:06:00,858
In a fighter this strong, it might have felt safe.

109
00:06:00,858 --> 00:06:02,899
It was a bet he could not win.

110
00:06:03,366 --> 00:06:06,914
Investigators later reconstructed it from the wreck.

111
00:06:06,914 --> 00:06:10,804
The throttle setting, the debris, the way the plane fell.

112
00:06:10,804 --> 00:06:14,966
Somewhere near twenty-five thousand feet, Mantell passed out.

113
00:06:15,433 --> 00:06:24,533
With a hand off the controls, the plane kept climbing for a while, up to around thirty thousand feet, before it fell off and started to turn.

114
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:26,453
Then came the spiral.

115
00:06:26,453 --> 00:06:29,567
Witnesses on the ground saw it circling down.

116
00:06:29,567 --> 00:06:31,366
The speed built and built.

117
00:06:31,833 --> 00:06:38,706
Near Franklin, Kentucky, on a farm, about four miles southwest of town, the fighter hit the ground.

118
00:06:38,706 --> 00:06:41,899
Mantell's watch had stopped at three eighteen.

119
00:06:42,366 --> 00:06:44,486
The accident report is plain.

120
00:06:44,486 --> 00:06:52,233
The plane had lost a wing in a dive, from too much speed, after the pilot blacked out from lack of oxygen.

121
00:06:52,699 --> 00:06:54,554
His seat belt was shredded.

122
00:06:54,554 --> 00:06:57,164
Word reached the tower within minutes.

123
00:06:57,164 --> 00:06:59,500
It was a terrible, ordinary crash.

124
00:06:59,966 --> 00:07:01,488
A young man was dead.

125
00:07:01,488 --> 00:07:03,227
A wife lost her husband.

126
00:07:03,227 --> 00:07:05,184
Two boys lost their father.

127
00:07:05,184 --> 00:07:08,300
And by dark, the newspapers had a headline.

128
00:07:08,766 --> 00:07:13,770
Quote, F-51 and Captain Mantell destroyed chasing flying saucer.

129
00:07:13,770 --> 00:07:17,523
The Louisville Courier printed it that same day.

130
00:07:17,523 --> 00:07:18,539
Not a theory.

131
00:07:18,539 --> 00:07:19,399
A headline.

132
00:07:19,866 --> 00:07:23,530
What happened next is a lesson in how a rumor grows.

133
00:07:23,530 --> 00:07:26,066
Within days, the crash was a legend.

134
00:07:26,533 --> 00:07:29,413
The rumors said a spacecraft shot him down.

135
00:07:29,413 --> 00:07:30,686
That a ray hit him.

136
00:07:30,686 --> 00:07:33,433
That small holes were found in the plane.

137
00:07:33,899 --> 00:07:36,914
They said his body was riddled with bullets.

138
00:07:36,914 --> 00:07:38,627
They said it was missing.

139
00:07:38,627 --> 00:07:41,161
That the plane came apart in mid-air.

140
00:07:41,161 --> 00:07:43,285
That the wreck was radioactive.

141
00:07:43,285 --> 00:07:46,299
Historian Curtis Peebles collected them all.

142
00:07:46,766 --> 00:07:50,007
Ruppelt wired for the real accident report.

143
00:07:50,007 --> 00:07:51,589
His summary is short.

144
00:07:51,589 --> 00:07:53,398
The body was not burned.

145
00:07:53,398 --> 00:07:55,282
It was not disintegrated.

146
00:07:55,282 --> 00:07:57,166
It was not full of holes.

147
00:07:57,633 --> 00:07:59,879
The wreck was not radioactive.

148
00:07:59,879 --> 00:08:01,526
It was not magnetized.

149
00:08:01,526 --> 00:08:05,195
A wing was gone because the plane dived too fast.

150
00:08:05,195 --> 00:08:07,666
There was no ray, and no gunfire.

151
00:08:08,133 --> 00:08:10,393
So the crash itself is explained.

152
00:08:10,393 --> 00:08:12,721
The mystery is the other question.

153
00:08:12,721 --> 00:08:14,090
What was he chasing?

154
00:08:14,090 --> 00:08:15,117
Three suspects.

155
00:08:15,117 --> 00:08:16,966
We start with the cheapest.

156
00:08:17,433 --> 00:08:20,899
Time for the first suspect: a planet, and a reflection.

157
00:08:21,366 --> 00:08:22,834
Suspect one: Venus.

158
00:08:22,834 --> 00:08:30,866
Project Sign, the Air Force's first UFO study, said so almost before the rescue crews reached the crash.

159
00:08:31,333 --> 00:08:32,593
There was a reason.

160
00:08:32,593 --> 00:08:35,776
Weeks earlier, a fighter pilot had chased Venus.

161
00:08:35,776 --> 00:08:44,199
And at three o'clock that afternoon, Venus was in the southwest, in nearly the right place, at about thirty-three degrees high.

162
00:08:44,666 --> 00:08:47,403
The tower guessed forty-five degrees.

163
00:08:47,403 --> 00:08:49,696
Close enough, with human error.

164
00:08:49,696 --> 00:08:51,471
But there was a problem.

165
00:08:51,471 --> 00:08:55,984
Venus was only about six times brighter than the daytime sky.

166
00:08:55,984 --> 00:08:57,833
Almost impossible to see.

167
00:08:58,299 --> 00:08:59,526
The astronomer J.

168
00:08:59,526 --> 00:09:01,907
Allen Hynek did the math himself.

169
00:09:01,907 --> 00:09:04,577
He had helped write the Venus answer.

170
00:09:04,577 --> 00:09:06,093
Then he took it back.

171
00:09:06,093 --> 00:09:11,000
Venus, he told Ruppelt, was not bright enough, and the day was hazy.

172
00:09:11,466 --> 00:09:18,129
That evening, at seven twenty, about a dozen towers did report a light low in the southwest.

173
00:09:18,129 --> 00:09:20,881
The Air Force says that one was Venus.

174
00:09:20,881 --> 00:09:21,243
Fine.

175
00:09:21,243 --> 00:09:23,633
But that was hours after Mantell.

176
00:09:24,100 --> 00:09:27,810
Suspect one and a half: a reflection on the canopy.

177
00:09:27,810 --> 00:09:30,357
A wingman said it, as he flew home.

178
00:09:30,357 --> 00:09:32,758
But Ruppelt asked a dozen pilots.

179
00:09:32,758 --> 00:09:36,033
A canopy flare fools you for a second or two.

180
00:09:36,500 --> 00:09:45,942
Mantell chased his object for something like fifteen to twenty minutes, while it was seen from the ground, with binoculars, at the same time.

181
00:09:45,942 --> 00:09:47,951
A reflection does not do that.

182
00:09:47,951 --> 00:09:51,299
A sundog has fuzzy edges and fades when you climb.

183
00:09:51,766 --> 00:09:56,903
So Venus is out as a full explanation, and the canopy and the sundog too.

184
00:09:56,903 --> 00:10:00,000
That leaves a stranger answer, and a secret.

185
00:10:00,466 --> 00:10:02,661
Suspect two: a Skyhook balloon.

186
00:10:02,661 --> 00:10:09,315
In nineteen forty-eight, the Navy and the Air Force were launching enormous research balloons.

187
00:10:09,315 --> 00:10:14,200
One hundred feet across, and rising to sixty thousand feet or higher.

188
00:10:14,666 --> 00:10:16,625
They were highly classified.

189
00:10:16,625 --> 00:10:18,794
Mantell had never heard of one.

190
00:10:18,794 --> 00:10:21,313
The men in the tower had not either.

191
00:10:21,313 --> 00:10:28,799
Seen from far away, in low winter sun, it would look like a parachute, or an ice cream cone with a red tip.

192
00:10:29,266 --> 00:10:33,606
In nineteen fifty-two, Ruppelt had the winds reconstructed.

193
00:10:33,606 --> 00:10:46,333
A balloon launched from Clinton County in Ohio could drift west, be seen at Maysville, then Owensboro, then slide south past Godman, at the height Mantell could never reach.

194
00:10:46,799 --> 00:10:50,299
And buried in the file were two forgotten reports.

195
00:10:50,299 --> 00:10:54,639
A man at Madisonville, looking through a telescope: a balloon.

196
00:10:54,639 --> 00:11:01,500
And an astronomer near Nashville that afternoon: a balloon, pear-shaped, with cables and a basket.

197
00:11:01,966 --> 00:11:03,352
But here is the gap.

198
00:11:03,352 --> 00:11:07,372
Nobody has ever found a launch record for January seventh.

199
00:11:07,372 --> 00:11:08,411
Ruppelt looked.

200
00:11:08,411 --> 00:11:11,669
Veterans remembered flying from Clinton County.

201
00:11:11,669 --> 00:11:14,233
They would not be pinned to that day.

202
00:11:14,700 --> 00:11:16,664
Critics add a second problem.

203
00:11:16,664 --> 00:11:21,136
That Nashville balloon was perhaps a hundred and fifty miles away.

204
00:11:21,136 --> 00:11:24,116
The balloon fits the descriptions very well.

205
00:11:24,116 --> 00:11:27,233
It does not, however, come with a paper trail.

206
00:11:27,700 --> 00:11:28,219
Debrief.

207
00:11:28,219 --> 00:11:30,233
Time to score the Mantell case.

208
00:11:30,700 --> 00:11:33,825
A real chase and a real crash: evidence.

209
00:11:33,825 --> 00:11:38,356
Many witnesses on the ground saw a large object: evidence.

210
00:11:38,356 --> 00:11:43,747
Mantell died from lack of oxygen: evidence, from the accident report.

211
00:11:43,747 --> 00:11:45,466
Venus alone: unlikely.

212
00:11:45,933 --> 00:11:49,809
A Skyhook balloon: the best fit, but still unproven.

213
00:11:49,809 --> 00:11:52,940
A ray, bullets, or a shoot-down: debunked.

214
00:11:52,940 --> 00:11:55,399
A spacecraft: no evidence at all.

215
00:11:55,866 --> 00:11:56,754
The verdict.

216
00:11:56,754 --> 00:11:58,604
Case closed on the death.

217
00:11:58,604 --> 00:12:01,119
Open on the object, but narrowing.

218
00:12:01,119 --> 00:12:08,666
Mantell died of hypoxia, chasing what was most likely a secret balloon he had no way of knowing about.

219
00:12:09,133 --> 00:12:12,327
The saddest part is that nothing alien is needed.

220
00:12:12,327 --> 00:12:17,867
A brave pilot, in a hurry to see something no one could name, went one rung too high.

221
00:12:17,867 --> 00:12:20,799
We still remember him by the thing he chased.

222
00:12:21,266 --> 00:12:29,302
Next case: that same year, over Fargo, a young pilot, a light that danced, and a dogfight that nobody could explain.

223
00:12:29,302 --> 00:12:30,133
Was it real?

224
00:12:30,600 --> 00:12:32,468
What do you think Mantell saw?

225
00:12:32,468 --> 00:12:37,700
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
